solution_steps:
We can use the Euclidean algorithm to find the greatest common divisor (GCD) of two numbers.
\begin{align*}
\text{gcd}(40304, 30203) &= \text{gcd}(30203, 40304 - 1 \cdot 30203) \\
&= \text{gcd}(30203, 10001).
\end{align*}
Next, we apply the Euclidean algorithm again:
\begin{align*}
\text{gcd}(30203, 10001) &= \text{gcd}(10001, 30203 - 3 \cdot 10001) \\
&= \text{gcd}(10001, 102).
\end{align*}
Once more, we apply the Euclidean algorithm:
\begin{align*}
\text{gcd}(10001, 102) &= \text{gcd}(102, 10001 - 98 \cdot 102) \\
&= \text{gcd}(102, 10001 - 98 \cdot 102) \\
&= \text{gcd}(102, 97).
\end{align*}
Finally, we find that $\text{gcd}(102, 97) = \text{gcd}(97, 102 - 1 \cdot 97) = \text{gcd}(97, 5) = \boxed{1}$.
The answer is: 1

